1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
So you want to write a book. I

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,200
know the feeling. I've been writing books for more

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,000
than 40 years. Now there's a lot

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,800
of people out there on the internet and

5
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:09,860
elsewhere that will try to tell you that

6
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:11,920
writing a book is easy. You can do it

7
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,510
fast. They've got five steps to writing

8
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:17,100
a bestseller. I'm not promising you a

9
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:19,500
bestseller, but I do have 13 foundational

10
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,900
steps that you're going to need to

11
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,340
follow if you're going to write a book.

12
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:26,780
Speed is not the point. Quality is the

13
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:28,820
point. So the first thing you're going

14
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:30,860
to want to do is establish your writing

15
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:34,400
space. When I first started, I was a young

16
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,940
father and I had no space. I had to take

17
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:39,940
a plank and put it between two kitchen

18
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:41,940
chairs, which I set in front of the couch

19
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:43,630
in the living room, and then I sat

20
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:45,320
on that couch in front of a manual

21
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,860
typewriter, and that's how I worked. That

22
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:50,400
was my space. Wherever it is, establish it.

23
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,660
It could be in a Starbucks. It could be

24
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:54,920
in your car if you have to. And you

25
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,760
should never say that you don't have a place

26
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,770
to write. I can remember being in

27
00:00:58,770 --> 00:01:00,780
the newspaper business. There were 40 of us

28
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:03,140
in the same room, and back in those

29
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:05,500
days people smoked in that room. So we had

30
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,120
smoke, we had noise, we had

31
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:10,740
clacking typewriters. A writer can write anywhere,

32
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:13,320
but you want to establish what you need.

33
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:15,900
So if you need solitude, make sure you

34
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:17,630
find a place in your house where you

35
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:19,360
can shut the door, where you can turn off

36
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:21,610
media, and you can have privacy and

37
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:23,860
silence and whatever you need to write.

38
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:26,580
And the more you can afford, the better you'll do,

39
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:26,660
especially if you're a writer. So if

40
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:26,740
you're a writer, you're going to need to

41
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:27,260
have a lot of space. You need to have a

42
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:27,760
lot of space. You need to have a lot of

43
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:28,260
space. So you can write in a room with

44
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:28,760
a lot of space, as far as equipment and

45
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:31,320
space. The second important step is to

46
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:33,880
assemble your writing tools. And really, you

47
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,820
don't need too many. Say you're writing

48
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:37,760
in a restaurant. All you need is your

49
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:39,930
laptop and a comfortable chair. Now, you

50
00:01:39,930 --> 00:01:42,100
may have to take whatever chair they've

51
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,340
got, but learn how that works for you. Do

52
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:46,580
you need to take a cushion from home so

53
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:48,680
you can sit up straight? Your back

54
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:50,780
and your neck are important to your writing.

55
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:52,550
You're going to be spending a lot of

56
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:54,320
hours in front of that computer. So don't

57
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:55,450
scrimp on your computer, and when

58
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:56,580
you're home, don't scrimp on your computer.

59
00:01:56,580 --> 00:01:58,150
Don't scrimp on your chair. And then make

60
00:01:58,150 --> 00:01:59,720
a list of all the things you're going to

61
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:01,580
need while you're writing, especially at home.

62
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:03,440
If you need paper clips or a stapler

63
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,690
or whatever, make sure you have all those

64
00:02:05,690 --> 00:02:07,940
within arm's length so you don't get distracted

65
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:10,139
by having to look for things when you need them.

66
00:02:10,139 --> 00:02:12,549
Okay, the third important thing you want to

67
00:02:12,549 --> 00:02:14,960
do is to break the project into small pieces.

68
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:17,910
The reason that writing a book seems so colossal

69
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:20,860
is because it is. Writing a book is akin to

70
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:23,630
eating an elephant. How do you eat an elephant?

71
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:26,400
One bite at a time. So break the task into

72
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,880
as many small pieces as you can. You have

73
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:31,360
to realize, yes, it's a four or five hundred

74
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:34,470
page manuscript in the end, but that's

75
00:02:34,470 --> 00:02:37,580
made up of sentences, paragraphs, chapters. Just

76
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:39,290
do them one at a time. That's

77
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:41,000
the way to get a handle on it.

78
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,430
Step number four is to settle on your big idea,

79
00:02:43,430 --> 00:02:45,860
and it needs to be a big idea. If it's

80
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:48,060
book worthy, it's going to be big concept.

81
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:50,260
We don't have any room in the marketplace

82
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:53,150
anymore for small concept book ideas. If it's small,

83
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:56,040
use it for a blog or an article. But

84
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:58,430
think how to win friends and influence

85
00:02:58,430 --> 00:03:00,820
people if you're thinking nonfiction. Think Harry

86
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:02,870
Potter if you're thinking fiction. It has

87
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:04,920
to be big. I can't overstate the importance

88
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:07,240
of this. If you try to write a book before

89
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,560
and you ran into a roadblock at the 20 or

90
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:12,810
30 day mark, or maybe the 20 or 30 page

91
00:03:12,810 --> 00:03:16,060
mark, it could be because your idea wasn't big enough.

92
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:18,630
How do you know if your idea is big enough

93
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:21,200
and if it has legs? If it stays with you.

94
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,190
If you tell your spouse or your friend what

95
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:25,180
your book is about, and every time you tell

96
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:25,599
them it gets bigger, it's going to be a big

97
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:26,019
idea. If you try to write a book before and

98
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:26,910
you ran into a roadblock at the 20 or

99
00:03:26,910 --> 00:03:27,800
30 page mark, that's a book that's going to last

100
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:30,410
in the marketplace too. Step five is to construct

101
00:03:30,410 --> 00:03:33,020
your outline. And, as you can see, I've done

102
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,630
that on my whiteboard here for you. Now,

103
00:03:35,630 --> 00:03:38,240
this might sound surprising for me to talk about

104
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,930
outlining when I'm known as a pantser, one

105
00:03:40,930 --> 00:03:43,620
who writes by the seat of his pants. Stephen

106
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:46,920
King is the best known writer who's a

107
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:50,220
pantser. He says, put interesting characters in difficult

108
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:52,890
situations and write to find out what happens. I

109
00:03:52,890 --> 00:03:55,560
like to do that, so people think I don't

110
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:55,980
outline a book.

111
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:58,000
I don't outline a book at all. But even

112
00:03:58,000 --> 00:03:59,980
we fiction writers who are pantsers have to have

113
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:02,510
some sort of idea where we're going. Even if

114
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:05,040
it's on one side of one sheet of paper,

115
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:07,430
give yourself some direction of where you're

116
00:04:07,430 --> 00:04:09,820
going. Now, some people, especially if you're

117
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:12,910
a beginning writer, your editor or your agent

118
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:16,000
may need to see an entire synopsis of your

119
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,550
novel idea. So you'll have to do more of

120
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:21,100
an outline than you might have to do later. And

121
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:23,550
agents and editors demand outlines for nonfiction.

122
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:26,000
There's no writing a nonfiction book without

123
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,720
an outline. They want to know what you're

124
00:04:27,720 --> 00:04:29,440
going to say, how you're going to say it,

125
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:31,780
where you're getting your information, and what

126
00:04:31,780 --> 00:04:34,120
your points are going to be. Now, we

127
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:36,280
often talk in fiction about the marathon of

128
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,440
the middle and how that stops everybody. That's

129
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:41,220
one of the places that I stop to. I

130
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:44,000
mean, I've written over 190 books and I every time

131
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,150
get to the halfway or three-quarter point and

132
00:04:46,150 --> 00:04:48,300
I wonder, why did I ever think I could

133
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:49,440
do this?

134
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,580
That's the marathon of the middle and you

135
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:53,720
can't just survive it or endure it. You have

136
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:55,540
to thrive in it.

137
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:58,370
Because the reader is right with you. If

138
00:04:58,370 --> 00:05:01,200
it seems boring to you, your reader is asleep.

139
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:03,170
So this happens to be true of nonfiction as

140
00:05:03,170 --> 00:05:05,140
well. Now, you'll take care of that with your

141
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,590
outline in nonfiction. You'll know that your middle

142
00:05:07,590 --> 00:05:10,040
has enough good stuff in it. In fiction,

143
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,180
especially if you're a pantser, you better be

144
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:14,320
sure you're saving a lot of big setups

145
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:17,290
and payoffs for that marathon of the middle.

146
00:05:17,290 --> 00:05:20,260
And you can do the same in nonfiction. The

147
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:22,780
same structure works for nonfiction as fiction.

148
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:25,300
You don't have the same number of elements.

149
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,850
As far as tension and conflict and dialogue

150
00:05:27,850 --> 00:05:30,400
and that type of thing. But you still need

151
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,660
the setup and the payoff. Make your

152
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:34,920
nonfiction book, say you're writing a nonfiction book

153
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:37,950
about how to build a model ship. You need

154
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:40,980
to set it up so that it looks impossible until

155
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:44,060
your specific solution comes through. That's

156
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:47,140
your setup and payoff. And remember, don't

157
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:49,390
be intimidated by an outline. Your outline serves

158
00:05:49,390 --> 00:05:51,640
you, not the other way around. If you've

159
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:53,300
got an outline and you find yourself drifting

160
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:54,960
from it or you think the book is working,

161
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:55,010
that's fine. But if you're not, you're not going

162
00:05:55,010 --> 00:05:55,060
to be able to do it. You're going to

163
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:55,120
have to work on it. And you're going to have

164
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:55,180
to work on it. And you're going to have to

165
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:55,220
work on it. And you're going to have to work

166
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:55,260
on it. And you're going to have to work on it.

167
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,640
I think you need to create a guideline about

168
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:58,020
how to move your outline to work in a different

169
00:05:58,020 --> 00:05:59,589
way, better. Change the outline. Don't change

170
00:05:59,589 --> 00:06:01,159
the book. Make your outline work for you.

171
00:06:01,159 --> 00:06:03,599
Okay, I'm back to my desk for point number

172
00:06:03,599 --> 00:06:06,039
six, and that is to set a firm writing schedule

173
00:06:06,039 --> 00:06:09,379
that includes a firm deadline that you keep

174
00:06:09,379 --> 00:06:12,719
sacred. This isn't a thing that hangs up too

175
00:06:12,719 --> 00:06:14,999
many beginning writers. They don't have a

176
00:06:14,999 --> 00:06:17,280
publisher's deadline. So they have to set

177
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:20,030
their own. And sometimes we tend to fudge

178
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:22,780
on our own deadlines. Make sure you don't

179
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:25,000
do that. Keep your deadline sacred.

180
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:26,860
And the way you do that is you figure

181
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:28,700
out roughly how many pages you're going to be

182
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:29,700
writing for your book.

183
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:32,779
If it's 300, 400, 500, divide that into

184
00:06:32,779 --> 00:06:35,859
the number of days you're allotting yourself to

185
00:06:35,859 --> 00:06:36,859
write.

186
00:06:36,859 --> 00:06:39,169
Now this may change once you get started and

187
00:06:39,169 --> 00:06:41,479
realize how many or how few pages you can

188
00:06:41,479 --> 00:06:43,039
write per day.

189
00:06:43,039 --> 00:06:44,849
If you schedule yourself for 10 pages a

190
00:06:44,849 --> 00:06:46,659
day and find that you're really not comfortable

191
00:06:46,659 --> 00:06:49,279
with more than four or five,

192
00:06:49,279 --> 00:06:51,900
change your schedule, change your deadline.

193
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:54,060
But once you get it locked in, keep it sacred.

194
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:57,440
When I was a publisher, I found that

195
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:00,820
only about one in 100 writers literally meet their

196
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:02,100
deadlines.

197
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:04,450
If you just do that, you set

198
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:06,800
yourself apart from 99 out of 100 writers.

199
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,420
Don't make the mistake of thinking you're

200
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:10,040
going to find the time to write.

201
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:12,250
When I have to write, I have

202
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:14,460
to have something sacrificed from my schedule.

203
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:16,600
Is it an hour or two of sleep a night?

204
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:17,600
Is it a concert?

205
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:18,600
Is it a ball game?

206
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:19,600
Is it a movie?

207
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:21,340
Is it a favorite TV show?

208
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:23,560
How bad do you want this?

209
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:24,040
I still have a lot of books.

210
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:26,160
I schedule my days right

211
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:28,280
on my calendar, on my computer.

212
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:29,280
I have it color coded.

213
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:30,280
It's in pink.

214
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:33,010
And you've caught me when tomorrow I

215
00:07:33,010 --> 00:07:35,740
have to reach the 70,000 word mark.

216
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:38,060
I have that target there.

217
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:41,070
I'm at the 67,500 mark now, so I'm

218
00:07:41,070 --> 00:07:44,080
going to have to write 2,500 words tomorrow.

219
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:46,580
And if I do that for five more days, I'm

220
00:07:46,580 --> 00:07:49,080
going to make my deadline because I keep that deadline

221
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:50,520
sacred.

222
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:52,080
Point number seven is to conduct your research.

223
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:52,580
Now, everybody knows that you

224
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:53,080
need to have a good schedule.

225
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:54,040
You need to have a good schedule.

226
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:57,100
You don't have to do that automatically for nonfiction.

227
00:07:57,100 --> 00:07:59,370
You have to be an expert in what you're

228
00:07:59,370 --> 00:08:01,640
writing in and not just draw in your own experience,

229
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:03,990
but also show that you've immersed yourself

230
00:08:03,990 --> 00:08:06,340
in all the writing in your field.

231
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:08,360
But a lot of people miss the fact

232
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:10,380
that research is just as important for fiction.

233
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:14,280
In fact, I think it could be even more important.

234
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:17,500
If you miss a small detail of history or

235
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:20,720
aircraft or weaponry, you can be sure readers are going

236
00:08:20,720 --> 00:08:23,020
to point this out.

237
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:24,020
Specificity lends credibility.

238
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:26,440
to fiction and fiction needs to be believable.

239
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:28,840
Now once you've done your research you're going

240
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:31,720
to be tempted to show that off to the reader.

241
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:34,600
You want to resist that urge. Your research is not your

242
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:37,480
main course. The story is the main course.

243
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:40,360
Research is the seasoning that adds that specificity that

244
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:43,000
gives you credibility and believability. I'm using

245
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,640
a world history chart for my current project

246
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:48,080
because I'm covering from 2000 BC to the present

247
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:50,520
day. I need to know when the patriarchs were born

248
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:53,200
and when they died and how they overlapped.

249
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:55,880
So make sure your research becomes seasoning and

250
00:08:55,880 --> 00:08:58,920
that it's right because readers notice. Step

251
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:01,960
number eight is to write a compelling

252
00:09:01,960 --> 00:09:04,040
reader first opener. Give it the time

253
00:09:04,040 --> 00:09:06,120
it deserves because if you can pull off

254
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:10,200
an important compelling first line it will set the

255
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:13,720
tone for your entire book. You probably won't write

256
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:17,000
a more important line than that first

257
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,720
one. Now most first lines fall into

258
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:20,390
one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight. And

259
00:09:20,390 --> 00:09:20,500
they're not the same. You're going to have to

260
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:24,380
read one of these categories. Surprising, dramatic

261
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:28,260
statement, philosophical, or poetic. I'm not going

262
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:30,900
to give you examples of all of them but let

263
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:33,540
me just do that for the first two. First surprising

264
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:37,860
opening in fiction. George Orwell's 1984 begins,

265
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:42,060
It was a bright cold day in April and

266
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:45,620
the clocks were striking 13. Would that keep you reading?

267
00:09:45,620 --> 00:09:47,940
It would me. In non-fiction

268
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:50,260
Elizabeth Gilbert wrote The Last American

269
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:53,020
Man. Her first line was, By the

270
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:55,780
time Eustace Conway was seven years old,

271
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:58,420
he could throw a knife accurately enough

272
00:09:58,420 --> 00:10:00,500
to nail a chipmunk to a tree.

273
00:10:01,939 --> 00:10:05,499
Now for dramatic statement, I'm sure you've

274
00:10:05,499 --> 00:10:09,060
read Toni Morrison's Paradise. Remember that first line?

275
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:12,420
They shoot the white girl first. That's a

276
00:10:12,420 --> 00:10:15,140
dramatic statement. Now let me read you the

277
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:17,580
opener to my work in progress. I'm not putting it

278
00:10:17,580 --> 00:10:20,020
in the category of these classics but here's the first one.

279
00:10:20,020 --> 00:10:21,460
It's your mother, Nicole Berman's father said

280
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:22,900
over the phone. She rose from her

281
00:10:23,319 --> 00:10:25,989
desk at the sound of his voice. What

282
00:10:25,989 --> 00:10:28,660
happened? Hopefully that'll keep you reading. Or can

283
00:10:29,699 --> 00:10:32,419
that вill keep you reading? Now what do I

284
00:10:32,419 --> 00:10:35,140
mean by reader first. Every decision you make in your

285
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:38,140
manuscript should go through that filter of reader

286
00:10:38,140 --> 00:10:41,140
first. Not you first. Not editor first. Not agent

287
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:44,520
first. Not reviewer first. Not critic first.

288
00:10:44,520 --> 00:10:47,900
Reader first. Whenever key readers write a he

289
00:10:47,900 --> 00:10:49,940
really liked a book of mine,

290
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:51,980
I think back to that motto,

291
00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:53,959
think reader first, that I often

292
00:10:53,959 --> 00:10:55,939
will tack right to my screen

293
00:10:55,939 --> 00:10:56,900
on a sticky note.

294
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:58,220
I want it to be

295
00:10:58,220 --> 00:10:59,540
the best, most compelling, most moving,

296
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:02,040
most emotional experience they've ever had,

297
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:05,660
because I'm thinking reader first, not anybody else first.

298
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:09,079
So think reader first, last, and always.

299
00:11:09,079 --> 00:11:11,480
Step number nine is to fill your story

300
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:13,660
with conflict and tension.

301
00:11:13,660 --> 00:11:15,860
Readers crave tension.

302
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:18,660
And yes, this applies to nonfiction as well.

303
00:11:18,660 --> 00:11:20,960
Almost every time a writer shows me their manuscript

304
00:11:20,960 --> 00:11:23,200
and says, I don't know where to go from here,

305
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:24,870
it's because they got to a

306
00:11:24,870 --> 00:11:26,540
point where the people on the page

307
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:28,940
are agreeing with each other too much.

308
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:30,420
And we like that in real life.

309
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:32,960
It's nice to have pleasant conversations,

310
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:34,500
talk with your spouse over a meal.

311
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:36,300
You're talking about how nice a day it is

312
00:11:36,300 --> 00:11:37,740
and what you're going to do.

313
00:11:37,740 --> 00:11:40,540
There's nothing more boring in fiction than that.

314
00:11:40,540 --> 00:11:43,700
So what you want to do is inject that conflict.

315
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:45,620
Have one of those characters say something

316
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:45,840
totally different.

317
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:47,240
Something totally off the wall.

318
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:48,940
Maybe one says, isn't it a beautiful day?

319
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:50,560
And the other one says,

320
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:52,180
oh, sure, you would say that.

321
00:11:52,180 --> 00:11:54,080
All of a sudden, the reader and that character

322
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:55,860
are going, what was that about?

323
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:56,900
Where did that come from?

324
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:58,400
That's conflict.

325
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:00,260
What's the problem in their relationship?

326
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:03,780
What's the underlying tension that caused that conflict?

327
00:12:03,780 --> 00:12:06,160
That will keep people turning the pages.

328
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:08,540
And you want to do that on every page,

329
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:11,340
even if it's just a matter of someone setting up

330
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:11,900
an appointment.

331
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,460
They need to see the doctor tomorrow.

332
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:15,800
There's an implication there that something's coming up,

333
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:18,180
otherwise why would the author put it in there?

334
00:12:18,180 --> 00:12:19,979
Now in nonfiction, how do you do that?

335
00:12:19,979 --> 00:12:21,560
You don't want unpleasantness.

336
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:23,180
It doesn't have to be something negative.

337
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:25,139
It doesn't have to be a

338
00:12:25,139 --> 00:12:27,099
battle or a war or a fight.

339
00:12:27,099 --> 00:12:29,520
Conflict and tension come up in nonfiction simply

340
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:31,979
by promising and then delivering,

341
00:12:31,979 --> 00:12:34,479
setting up and paying off.

342
00:12:34,479 --> 00:12:36,300
Some of the best nonfiction writers

343
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:39,920
are ones who spend the first several chapters promising you

344
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:41,580
what you're going to get

345
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:43,240
when you finish reading this book,

346
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:45,260
and then they deliver.

347
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:47,700
Step number 10 is to turn off your internal editor

348
00:12:47,700 --> 00:12:50,340
while you're writing your first draft.

349
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:52,620
Most writers I know are perfectionists.

350
00:12:52,620 --> 00:12:54,400
I happen to be one, too.

351
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:55,940
And so we have that

352
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:57,480
inner critic sitting on our shoulder,

353
00:12:57,480 --> 00:13:00,340
telling us what's wrong with every word we write.

354
00:13:00,340 --> 00:13:03,880
That inner critic is just you or me,

355
00:13:03,880 --> 00:13:07,439
and that critic needs to be told to shut up.

356
00:13:07,439 --> 00:13:09,169
Now is not the time

357
00:13:09,169 --> 00:13:10,900
to be criticizing your own work.

358
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:14,180
Always save your editing until the next day, at least.

359
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:14,430
And the longer you can hold it up, the

360
00:13:14,430 --> 00:13:14,680
moreotypes happen, and I'm glad to have that set up.

361
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:16,639
The longer you can wait between when you write

362
00:13:16,639 --> 00:13:18,599
it and when you edit it, the better for the

363
00:13:18,599 --> 00:13:20,219
end product.

364
00:13:20,219 --> 00:13:23,059
This is the opening pages of my work

365
00:13:23,059 --> 00:13:25,900
in progress, Dead Sea Rising, my next novel.

366
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:29,040
I wouldn't show this first draft to my worst enemy.

367
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:34,140
I don't worry about cliches, redundancies, lacks of logic.

368
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:36,560
I need to get the story down.

369
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:38,790
So turn off that internal editor, get your

370
00:13:38,790 --> 00:13:41,020
story down, and then tell yourself that the

371
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:43,820
next day you can put your perfectionist

372
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:46,620
cap back on and have at it.

373
00:13:46,620 --> 00:13:48,159
Remember in point number five when

374
00:13:48,159 --> 00:13:49,699
I mentioned the Marathon of the Middle?

375
00:13:49,699 --> 00:13:51,859
I want to make that point 11 and

376
00:13:51,859 --> 00:13:54,020
hit that again because if there's any place you're

377
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:56,380
going to quit, it's going to

378
00:13:56,380 --> 00:13:58,740
be during the Marathon of the Middle.

379
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:01,280
This is the toughest spot for me as well.

380
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:04,380
I've written over 190 books in 40 years.

381
00:14:04,380 --> 00:14:07,420
I hit the Marathon of the Middle every time

382
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:10,460
and I wonder why did I get into this business?

383
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:10,960
Okay.

384
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:13,270
The problem with the Marathon of the Middle

385
00:14:13,270 --> 00:14:15,580
is we've all got great ideas to start and

386
00:14:15,580 --> 00:14:17,760
we can't wait to get to that big finish,

387
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:19,940
but now we've got a couple hundred pages in the

388
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:20,940
middle to fill.

389
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:23,550
And if you just start padding it in

390
00:14:23,550 --> 00:14:26,160
fiction with extra scenes or nonfiction with extra

391
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:30,780
points, your reader is going to drop off the page.

392
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:35,180
This is where you don't just survive, you thrive.

393
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:37,790
For instance, in my current work in progress, Dead

394
00:14:37,790 --> 00:14:40,400
Sea Rising, this is a long novel, 80,000 words.

395
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:43,660
So the Marathon of the Middle is a good stretch.

396
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:46,590
How I solved the problem of not letting it

397
00:14:46,590 --> 00:14:49,520
flag in the middle is to alternate from 2000

398
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:53,260
B.C. to present day and even back to Vietnam.

399
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:55,750
And I'm setting up my payoffs so well in the

400
00:14:55,750 --> 00:14:58,240
middle that I can hardly wait to get to the

401
00:14:58,240 --> 00:14:59,240
ending.

402
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:01,440
And the ending will work better because

403
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:03,640
I didn't just persevere through the Marathon,

404
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:05,819
I thrived.

405
00:15:05,819 --> 00:15:08,780
Step number 12 is to write a resounding ending.

406
00:15:08,780 --> 00:15:10,220
You want your book to end.

407
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:13,160
The way a Broadway play ends, when

408
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:15,920
that curtain comes down with a satisfying thud.

409
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:19,050
I'm working on my 195th book, so I've got

410
00:15:19,050 --> 00:15:22,180
over 190 books here that all had to have endings

411
00:15:22,180 --> 00:15:23,319
that worked.

412
00:15:23,319 --> 00:15:25,869
Two thirds of my books are novels, one

413
00:15:25,869 --> 00:15:28,420
third are nonfiction, so even nonfiction has to

414
00:15:28,420 --> 00:15:30,840
have that great ending.

415
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:33,079
How do you make sure your ending doesn't fizzle?

416
00:15:33,079 --> 00:15:35,079
You give it the time it deserves.

417
00:15:35,079 --> 00:15:37,139
I had talked to a lot of writers who've

418
00:15:37,139 --> 00:15:39,199
written their entire manuscript, they get to the end,

419
00:15:39,199 --> 00:15:40,199
and they rush it.

420
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:42,220
Or they say, I just don't

421
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:44,040
know how to make it work.

422
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:46,199
Don't settle for second best.

423
00:15:46,199 --> 00:15:48,319
If it takes longer to write your ending than

424
00:15:48,319 --> 00:15:50,439
the rest of the novel put together, or the

425
00:15:50,439 --> 00:15:52,980
nonfiction book put together, do it.

426
00:15:52,980 --> 00:15:55,240
Do whatever it takes to make it work.

427
00:15:55,240 --> 00:15:58,650
And if you've got several ideas for what might be

428
00:15:58,650 --> 00:16:02,060
best, go for the one that is the most emotional.

429
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:05,500
Because readers remember what moves them.

430
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:06,409
My last and most important point, step 13,

431
00:16:06,409 --> 00:16:07,319
is that you need to become a first-person reader.

432
00:16:07,319 --> 00:16:08,319
I'm going to give you a few tips.

433
00:16:08,319 --> 00:16:09,319
Step 13 is to write a resounding ending.

434
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:12,920
You need to become a ferocious self-editor.

435
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:14,180
What does it mean to be ferocious?

436
00:16:14,180 --> 00:16:15,180
You know what it means.

437
00:16:15,180 --> 00:16:17,260
It means to be aggressive.

438
00:16:17,260 --> 00:16:19,910
Everything else is for naught if you

439
00:16:19,910 --> 00:16:22,560
don't polish your manuscript to the point where

440
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:24,720
you're happy with every word.

441
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,270
That doesn't mean it's going to be perfect or

442
00:16:26,270 --> 00:16:27,820
that you don't need an editor if you should

443
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:30,120
place it with a publisher.

444
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:33,960
But you need to polish that thing until it sings.

445
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:34,960
Why?

446
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:38,960
Because agents and editors can tell within two minutes.

447
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:42,180
Whether your manuscript is going

448
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:45,040
to be worth reading or rejecting.

449
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:47,400
That doesn't sound fair and maybe it isn't

450
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:49,760
fair, but they have so many things to read.

451
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:52,740
The competition is so vast.

452
00:16:52,740 --> 00:16:55,170
They've learned to be able to tell within

453
00:16:55,170 --> 00:16:57,600
a page or two whether this has potential or

454
00:16:57,600 --> 00:16:59,060
not.

455
00:16:59,060 --> 00:17:03,840
That puts all the onus on you to self-edit.

456
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,520
People say, should I pay an editor?

457
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:08,160
If you pay an editor, what is the publisher buying?

458
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:09,160
Your work or someone else's?

459
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:12,420
Learn to edit yourself.

460
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:14,540
Cut to add power.

461
00:17:14,540 --> 00:17:16,839
I have a list of 21 self-editing tips.

462
00:17:16,839 --> 00:17:18,980
You can find them at jerryjenkins.com.

463
00:17:18,980 --> 00:17:20,619
I've been so blessed in my career

464
00:17:20,619 --> 00:17:22,260
that I love to pay it forward.

465
00:17:22,260 --> 00:17:25,619
So I'm sending out free writing tips and writing blogs.

466
00:17:25,619 --> 00:17:27,260
You can find all that at that

467
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:28,900
website and you'll be ready to go.

468
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:29,900
The End

469
00:17:29,900 --> 00:17:30,900
The End

470
00:17:30,900 --> 00:17:31,900
The End

471
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:32,900
The End

472
00:17:32,900 --> 00:17:33,900
The End

473
00:17:33,900 --> 00:17:34,900
The End

474
00:17:34,900 --> 00:17:35,614
The End

